[Music]  along florida's space coast on the  atlantic ocean much of the attention is  focused on flight and rockets lifting  off from kennedy space center and cape  canaveral but nearby scientists have a  much more terrestrial concern how to  preserve a once thriving population of  oysters vital to the health of one of  the state's most ecologically important  waterways their effort is being  complicated by climate change lonnie  quinn reports  this is mosquito lagoon  it is a picture of tranquility  dolphins swimming by  and wading birds just watching over  things  many say it is the most biologically  diverse estuary on  the mainland united states  linda walters would know the university  of central florida professor has been  studying these waters for 24 years with  a focus on the eastern oyster they're  superstars there is no question about  that  they can handle all sorts of climatic  conditions do you think people around  the world don't have an appreciation for  an oyster i think everybody could do a  little bit better in appreciating  oysters they're not the prettiest animal  in the ocean  they don't have to be pretty they just  have to do a really important job  that job is filtering water  yes they look like rocks but these are  actually clusters of oysters  and each oyster sucks in 20 gallons of  water every day filled with algae and  muck and eventually spits out cleaner  and clearer water  everybody should care about oysters it's  just that simple they are one of the  most important species on this planet  for keeping our water clean  i see why you told me to wear an old  pair of sneakers these things are like  razor blades since 1943 mosquito lagoon  has lost 40 percent of its oyster reefs  within the boundaries of canaveral  national seashore  much of that due to boat wakes that  dislodge the oysters i see the healthy  part of the oyster reef which is this  dark color and right here where it's  white this is dead correct that's dead  and that's that's boat wake impact  walters had to help she and her team  have restored more than six percent of  the reefs but now  there's a new threat  mangroves are encroaching the  consequence of warming waters  it gets probably close to 95 96 in these  sorts of yeah 95 degrees  let me tell you linda we put bubbles in  here we got a jacuzzi  and that's how the mangrove trees prefer  it red mangroves will die when the air  temperatures dip below 24 degrees that's  known as a hard freeze and it prevents  the trees from moving to the north  but this area has not had one of those  in decades now over the last 20 years  the oysters in the mangroves are  competing for the same land and the  oysters are losing that battle this is a  dead reef but the mangroves they have  blown up they have gone up by 198  percent  now that may not be a bad thing  mangroves remove carbon from the air  they also provide a buffer during storms  but in this turf war professor walters  is rooting for the oyster  so in terms of climate change we always  talk about the average mean temperature  increasing but for you you're focusing  on that morning low temperature not  getting down as cold as it's been in the  past yeah that is a new field of  research that's called tropicalization  and we're finding it for lots of plants  lots of animals a single mangrove tree  produces hundreds to thousands of  propagules these are pre-germinated  seeds that look a little like a green  bean they float on the water and can  root on an oyster reef what you can see  right here is a new propagule so this  would have just shown up here and gotten  trapped amongst these clusters probably  within the last two weeks and this other  one here  is probably 20 years old at this point  and you can see that other new mangroves  are starting to grow right underneath  this they are just going to take up more  and more of the oyster reef as they all  start growing it looks like they coexist  nicely but the mangroves may be causing  the oysters to die off  ucf graduate student katherine harris  says it's likely it has something to do  with the chemistry of the trees  mangroves basically contribute to much  more acidic levels in the sediment and  over time that maybe starts to dissolve  the oyster shells and weaken the shells  which makes you know the base that the  oysters will grow on go away  one solution is for man to step in and  make more bases  this restored reef is a series of grids  that are made believe it or not from  potato chip waste so that's that's made  out of potatoes that's made out of  potatoes it is a starch material that is  considered a bioplastic  and these containers they shore up the  reef each one is filled with about 30  pounds of recycled oyster shells from  restaurants so we're keeping all of the  oysters out of landfills while creating  new oyster habitats and putting them  back out into the lagoon jessie wales of  the marine discovery center says the  recycled oyster shells act like a magnet  and attract tiny baby oysters to latch  on  since oysters are made out of calcium  carbonate as they leach off that calcium  carbonate baby oysters can sense it in  the water and they will actually find  these oyster bags and attach onto them  now what pile are we are we digging from  it's a simple process  that kind of doubles as cardio  go ahead and dump that baby right in  keep shaking it out camping on the  ground and we only need 1 399 more to go  to date the marine discovery center has  recycled more than 700 000 pounds of  oyster shells this entire back parking  lot is going to be full of oyster bags  by the time we're done  the center also restored this marsh now  they intended for it to be a marsh grass  nursery but mangroves took over here too  they got so thick we had to back out  where do the mangroves stop like they  don't go farther north than where  if you look at a textbook that's like 10  years old right here in mesquite lagoon  used to be the stop point for mangroves  at least for one species of mangoes red  mangroves and it used to be st augustine  which is about  70 miles north of here for black  mangroves now you see both species up  into georgia  where do you think they could be let's  say 10 years from now 10 years from now  i think north and south carolina are  going to have a lot more mangroves than  they do now which they pretty much have  none right now  and you will see the propagules the  seeds floating in the water much further  north because they won't be dying on the  way there  now no one can say for sure what that  will mean for coastal areas in the mid  and the north atlantic but right here in  mosquito lagoon  professor walters she finds plenty of  hope well with everything that's taken  place out here and having spent my  entire day with you you haven't stopped  smiling the whole day  for some reason you feel good about  things not bad about things  you know if you're gonna feel bad about  it you just have to find another job  there is so much willpower in  the united states and around the globe  to make our estuaries better lots of  people want to understand the problem  they want to help the problem and they  want to correct it for the future  generations is it fair to call you the  the oyster whisperer for cbs this  morning saturday i've been called the  oyster lady before ladies whether that's  nice or not ronnie quinn mosquito lagoon  florida  whenever we see stories like this i'm so  glad there are people that are so much  smarter than i am to figure something  out i always thought mangroves were a  good thing because they provided  protection against these battering  storms right yes it's where they are  though yeah that's what matters oyster  whisper i prefer oyster eater  there it is well i like that too we'll  take that

